Inspect Curry objects from Python
=================================

This example looks inside a loaded Curry module with the module curry.inspect.
It lists the symbols and the types of the module Nat, finds the files that
hold its ICurry, and prints the code that the backend generated for one
function.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

The module Nat.curry defines the natural numbers as data and two conversion
functions.  The example shows what Sprite makes of such a module: ICurry,
the intermediate language of the Curry front end, and the code of the
backend.  The generated code for natToInt shows the shape of a Curry
function in Sprite.  It brings its argument to head normal form, selects on
the constructor, and rewrites the root of the expression.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The script adds its own directory to curry.path and imports the module Nat.
Then it calls these functions of curry.inspect:

    inspect.symbols(Nat)        the public constructors and functions
    inspect.types(Nat)          the types
    inspect.geticurryfile(Nat)  the ICurry file of the module
    inspect.getjsonfile(Nat)    the ICurry-JSON file of the module
    inspect.getimpl(Nat.natToInt)  the generated code of one function

Types are not attributes of the module object.  The script looks the type up
by its full name, curry.type('Nat.Nat'), and reads its constructors from the
type object.

The symbol list holds two names that are not in the source:
natFromInt_COMPLEXCASE0 and natFromInt_COMPLEXCASE1.  The front end lifts
the guarded rules of natFromInt into these helper functions.

The script prints the names of the ICurry files and the first line of the
generated code on the standard output.  The whole generated code goes to
the standard error stream.  It changes with the compiler, and expected.out
holds the standard output only.


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

The run script adds interpret:off to the interpreter flags, so the module
is compiled before it runs, and inspect.getimpl reads the generated C++
function from the .cpp file beside the shared library.  That is the code
on the standard error stream, which section 2 describes.  Under the
default of the flag interpret (tiered) a fresh module is interpreted from
its ICurry first, and getimpl shows its bytecode there instead.  The first
line is the banner "/****** Nat.natToInt ******/" under every mode, and
whatever the C++ compiler, so expected.out holds it.  The example runs on
the C++ backend.


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the exact output of ./run:

    % python go.py
    Symbols defined in Nat: I, O, natFromInt, natFromInt_COMPLEXCASE0, natFromInt_COMPLEXCASE1, natToInt
    Types defined in Nat: Nat
    Constructors of Nat.Nat: O (arity 0), I (arity 1)

    ICurry file: Nat.icy
    ICurry-JSON file: Nat.json.z
    Both live under .curry/ next to Nat.curry.

    The C++ implementation of Nat.natToInt starts with:
        /****** Nat.natToInt ******/
    The whole code is on the standard error stream.

The front end writes the ICurry files under .curry/ next to the source, in a
subdirectory named after the front end version.  The whole generated code
follows on the standard error stream.  It is a C++ function of about twenty
lines.  It brings the argument to head normal form, selects on its
constructor, and rewrites the root with 0 for O and with 1 plus the
converted predecessor for I.


5. Limits
---------

A warm run of ./run took 0.3 s.  The first run also compiles the module
with the Curry front end and the C++ compiler, which takes about 2 s
more.  Only the first line of the generated code is part of expected.out,
so a change of the generated code does not change the expected output.
